# pyenv
#   For a library or package, you might want to comit it.
#   For an application, you might want to ignore it.
#.python-version

# pipenv
#   According to pypa/pipenv#1173, pipenv managed virtual environments are placed in
#   ~/.local/share/virtualenvs by default. But if you have PIPENV_VENV_IN_PROJECT=1
#   set, they will be created in the project directory as .venv.
#   For a library/package, you should check Pipfile.lock into source control.
#   For an application, you should check Pipfile.lock into source control.
#Pipfile.lock

# poetry
#   Similar to pipenv, poetry managed virtual environments can be in project
#Poetry.lock

# pdm
#   PDM stores project-wide configurations in .pdm.toml
.pdm.toml
#   PDM can optionally create a virtualenv named .venv in the project root
.venv/

